## Polaris RZR vs Can-Am at a glance

| | Polaris RZR | Can-Am Maverick |
|---|---|---|
| Who it suits | First-timers and relaxed drivers | Drivers who have been in a buggy before |
| Models we run | 5 | 6 |
| Cheapest 30 minutes | AED 300 | AED 550 |
| Cheapest hour | AED 550 | AED 999 |
| Four seat version | Yes | Yes |
| Minimum driver age | 14 | 14 |

Both brands leave from the same Al Awir base, follow the same guided route into the Lahbab red dunes and include the same helmet, briefing, fuel, water and lead guide. The brand changes the machine, not the trip.

## How the two brands actually drive on sand

### Polaris RZR: forgiving, and honest about it

The **Polaris RZR** sits a little taller and feels calmer at low speed. When a beginner hesitates halfway up a dune face or brakes too hard on the way down, the RZR tends to shrug it off. That is exactly what a first drive needs. The [Polaris RZR 1000 two seater](/dune-buggy-dubai/polaris-rzr-1000-2-seater/) has enough power for the big red dunes and is the machine most of our guests start in.

The RZR Turbo adds boost for drivers who want quicker acceleration out of the bowls, and the RZR Pro brings a longer travel suspension for people who already know how sand behaves.

### Can-Am Maverick: lower, wider, and it wants commitment

The **Can-Am Maverick** range sits lower and wider, and every one we run is turbocharged. On a dune it rewards a driver who picks a line and holds the throttle through it. It punishes the opposite. A nervous driver in a Can-Am spends the slot lifting off at the wrong moment, and the machine feels twitchy for reasons that have nothing to do with the machine.

The [Can-Am Maverick X3](/dune-buggy-dubai/can-am-maverick-x3-2-seater/) is the step up from a Polaris. Above it sit the X3 Turbo RR and the Maverick R, which our [Can-Am X3 vs Maverick R comparison](/blogs/can-am-x3-vs-maverick-r-dubai/) covers in detail.

## What each brand costs per buggy

Every figure is per buggy, not per person.

| Buggy | Seats | 30 minutes | 1 hour | 2 hours |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Polaris RZR 1000 | 2 | AED 300 | AED 550 | AED 999 |
| Polaris RZR 1000 | 4 | AED 350 | AED 550 | AED 1099 |
| Polaris RZR Turbo | 2 | AED 450 | AED 800 | AED 1550 |
| Polaris RZR Turbo | 4 | AED 450 | AED 850 | AED 1650 |
| Polaris RZR Pro | 2 | AED 500 | AED 900 | AED 1700 |
| Can-Am Maverick X3 | 2 | AED 550 | AED 999 | AED 1750 |
| Can-Am Maverick X3 | 4 | AED 600 | AED 999 | AED 1800 |
| Can-Am X3 Turbo RR | 2 | AED 650 | AED 1200 | AED 1900 |
| Can-Am X3 Turbo RR | 4 | AED 700 | AED 1300 | AED 2000 |
| Can-Am Maverick R | 2 | AED 800 | AED 1500 | AED 2300 |
| Can-Am Maverick R | 4 | AED 850 | AED 1500 | AED 2350 |

The line worth reading twice is AED 550. That buys a full hour in the Polaris RZR 1000 or 30 minutes in the cheapest Can-Am. The other is the four seat Polaris RZR 1000, which costs the same AED 550 for an hour as the two seater, so a group of four never needs two buggies to save money.

## Which brand to book

### Book a Polaris if

- nobody in the group has driven a dune buggy before
- you want the full hour rather than a short taster
- the driver is a teenager or a nervous adult
- the budget matters and four people want to share one buggy

### Book a Can-Am if

- the driver has done at least one buggy ride and enjoyed it
- you want the most power the slot can buy
- you are booking an hour or longer, so there is time to use that power

If you are somewhere between the two, the Polaris RZR Turbo is the honest middle answer: more boost than the RZR 1000, and AED 800 for an hour against AED 999 for the cheapest Can-Am.

## What we see at the base

Most weeks someone books a Can-Am for their first ever buggy ride, drives it carefully for twenty minutes and tells us afterwards they wish they had taken the hour in the Polaris. We rarely hear the reverse. The Can-Am is a better machine for a driver who can use it. For a driver who cannot yet, it is a more expensive way to go slowly.

That is why the warm-up matters. Every session starts on flat sand, where the guide watches throttle and steering before anyone moves onto the dunes. If a driver is not ready for the machine they booked, we move them to one that suits them on the same slot. Drivers must be 14 or older, and no licence is needed on our private desert routes.

## Booking either brand

Message us on WhatsApp with the date, the group size and who is driving. We reply with availability and the total in AED, usually within minutes. There is no deposit on standard slots, payment is cash, card or bank transfer at the base, and cancelling more than 24 hours ahead is free.

Driving yourself to the Al Awir base costs nothing. Pickup and drop-off inside Dubai and Sharjah is an optional AED 300, and transfers from the other emirates are quoted with the booking. We have run this base since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests into the dunes, and the [full dune buggy fleet](/dune-buggy-dubai/) shows every Polaris and Can-Am side by side.
